From: Marionette AirDrop driver research
Subject: [EXPERIMENTAL] allow long station ROC dwells for AWDL

The 200 ms clamp on a station remain-on-channel request is our own code, not a
firmware limit. wiphy advertises MAX_REMAIN_ON_CHANNEL_DURATION (2000), and the
0045 experiment measured a 2 s station ROC with 0 % packet loss (latency
14 ms -> 52 ms). A 200 ms dwell plus the driver's ~120 ms READY latency makes
the radio deaf for ~38 % of every cycle, which collapses bulk TCP transfers
(measured 2026-11-05: 16-43 KiB/s, RTO plateaus up to 19.8 s).

Raise the clamp to the wiphy maximum so userspace can request long dwells that
amortise the acquisition cost, and let the promiscuous AWDL receive filter
cover the same range (0008 previously stopped at 1000 ms).

Measured with a 1800 ms userspace request against this 2000 ms cap:
  - "ROC request failed": 0
  - dwell cycle ~1.9 s (~95 % duty) instead of ~330 ms (~62 %)
  - STA stays associated (ping replies continue, latency ~0.9 s while a
    transfer runs, which is the time-sharing cost)
  - 1 MiB 11.0 s, 8 MiB 116.5 s, 32 MiB 358.9 s; all phone-side SHA256s match
    the sender

Build/deploy used for that measurement (yuni):
  DRIVER_TARGET=modules DRIVER_JOBS=8 \
  DRIVER_PROVIDER_SYMVERS=$PWD/stock-provider.symvers \
    bash driver/build-baseline.sh kernel kernel-out wlan clang
  ./clang/bin/llvm-objcopy --strip-debug \
    wlan/qcacld-3.0/kiwi_v2.ko audit-0059/candidate-0059.ko
  candidate-0059.ko sha256 8570940428b10acd6e75dd76b052af8b0b155b72ec3fd4b7453b234fc1e63562
  (24 141 624 bytes; 24 bytes differ from candidate-0058)

The on-device loader keeps its capability fast-path, so replacing a loaded
0058 with 0059 needs a forced run: copy the modules plus driver-load.sh with
the `if [ -f "$CAPTURE_HOLD" ]` block removed and run that copy as root.

